WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.301
-Our first guest tonight

00:00:02.301 --> 00:00:05.004
is an Academy Award-nominated
actor you know him for his work

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:06.806
in "Spotlight" and "Birdman"

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:10.209
as well as iconic films such as
"Batman" and "Beetlejuice."

00:00:10.209 --> 00:00:12.244
Don't sleep on "Multiplicity"
or "The Paper."

00:00:12.244 --> 00:00:13.847
I'm gonna throw
a couple more in there.

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:15.681
He stars in "Worth,"
which begins streaming

00:00:15.681 --> 00:00:18.484
on Netflix September 3rd.
Let's take a look.

00:00:18.484 --> 00:00:23.122
-I've got -- what? --
1,100 families on board

00:00:23.122 --> 00:00:26.626
and 6,000 more to go.

00:00:26.626 --> 00:00:30.563
-Uh-huh, yeah.
-Well, I just want you to know

00:00:30.563 --> 00:00:34.867
that I know
the fund's not perfect.

00:00:34.867 --> 00:00:37.369
There are people
who just don't fit

00:00:37.369 --> 00:00:39.406
within the rules that we wrote.

00:00:41.641 --> 00:00:46.179
I know that.

00:00:46.179 --> 00:00:51.017
I honestly
don't know what to do.

00:00:51.017 --> 00:00:53.352
-Please welcome back to the show
our friend Michael Keaton.

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:55.688
How are you, Michael?
-I'm good, man.

00:00:55.688 --> 00:00:57.189
Nice to see you.

00:00:57.189 --> 00:00:58.624
-Yeah, it's nice
to see you, too.

00:00:58.624 --> 00:01:00.826
You know,
I wish it wasn't on Zoom.

00:01:00.826 --> 00:01:02.795
-Well, you know, I know.
Hold on a sec.

00:01:02.795 --> 00:01:06.799
I'm getting a call.
-Getting a call?

00:01:06.799 --> 00:01:09.101
-No.
I'm texting Jimmy Fallon.

00:01:09.101 --> 00:01:10.603
-Oh, yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:11.904
-Yeah.
-That's cool.

00:01:11.904 --> 00:01:13.539
Yeah, I'm glad -- I'm glad
you guys get a long.

00:01:13.539 --> 00:01:15.208
-Anyways, sorry.
-'Cause, you know,

00:01:15.208 --> 00:01:16.910
I would have thought --
and one thing if, like,

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.410
you don't leave Montana,
I get it.

00:01:18.410 --> 00:01:21.414
But last week, look at you two.

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.516
Thick as thieves.
-Yeah.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:25.752
-Laughing it up.
Look how happy he is.

00:01:25.752 --> 00:01:27.020
-I know.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:29.822
-No one's ever reacted like that
to a Zoom interview.

00:01:29.822 --> 00:01:31.991
[ Laughter ]

00:01:31.991 --> 00:01:34.560
-That's true.
That's true.

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:36.496
That's true.
Yeah.

00:01:36.496 --> 00:01:37.997
Give me a minute.
I miss --

00:01:37.997 --> 00:01:39.565
It was only a week ago, I guess.

00:01:39.565 --> 00:01:42.535
Let me reminisce a little bit.
I missed it so much.

00:01:42.535 --> 00:01:45.338
[ Laughter ]

00:01:45.338 --> 00:01:47.140
-It is great to see you.
-That's true, though.

00:01:47.140 --> 00:01:48.807
That's true, though.
That's very funny.

00:01:48.807 --> 00:01:50.510
No, you're right.
People don't --

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:53.412
You can't do that on a Zoom.
There's so much you can't do.

00:01:53.412 --> 00:01:55.514
Honestly,
I did want to be there.

00:01:55.514 --> 00:01:57.349
It was my fault.
I kind of screwed up.

00:01:57.349 --> 00:02:01.487
I thought only --
We could only do this via Zoom.

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:06.960
Plus, I have to tell you,
they give me pie when I'm there.

00:02:06.960 --> 00:02:08.461
-At the Fallon show?
At "The Tonight Show"?

00:02:08.461 --> 00:02:11.330
-Yeah.
Yeah.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:14.801
-Is that all it takes?
[ Laughter ]

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.703
-Well, I like pie, and --
I don't know.

00:02:17.703 --> 00:02:20.172
It's just
never been offered to me.

00:02:20.172 --> 00:02:22.608
You guys have had --
-You think if we didn't

00:02:22.608 --> 00:02:24.977
know that
we wouldn't get you a pie?

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:26.879
-I don't know.
-You're Michael Keaton.

00:02:26.879 --> 00:02:29.181
-I didn't even ask.
They just kind of said,

00:02:29.181 --> 00:02:31.484
"Hey, Mike, you like some pie?"

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:34.487
[ Laughter ]
-I said, "[Bleep] yeah.

00:02:34.487 --> 00:02:35.855
I love some pie."

00:02:35.855 --> 00:02:37.757
I'm a pie man
more than a cake man.

00:02:37.757 --> 00:02:40.993
I'll eat cake,
but pie is my preference.

00:02:40.993 --> 00:02:43.096
Anyway, I'm sorry.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:43.096 --> 00:02:45.264
Sorry it didn't work out.
-No, I get it.

00:02:45.264 --> 00:02:47.533
And, by the way, my feelings --
It was only that you were here

00:02:47.533 --> 00:02:49.535
that I -- you know, I was like,
"Oh, he comes here,"

00:02:49.535 --> 00:02:51.170
because you were in Montana.
-Yeah.

00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:54.474
-I do -- I have enjoyed that
our Zooms during this time,

00:02:54.474 --> 00:02:57.276
I'm getting to see other corners
of your beautiful home there.

00:02:57.276 --> 00:02:59.445
-Yeah.
-But you are like --

00:02:59.445 --> 00:03:01.681
You're an actual horseman.
Is that the right way --

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:03.782
You, like,
will ride horses out there?

00:03:03.782 --> 00:03:08.754
-Yeah, yeah. No, no -- horseman
is a good way to put it.

00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:10.656
Yeah, I do.
I do.

00:03:10.656 --> 00:03:13.792
I'm, you know --
If I would open the window,

00:03:13.792 --> 00:03:17.163
I would look at them now.

00:03:17.163 --> 00:03:20.733
But what I was thinking about
was maybe you might want

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:22.835
to think about it is,
when we're done,

00:03:22.835 --> 00:03:26.272
just get somebody to photograph
you throwing your head back,

00:03:26.272 --> 00:03:30.909
laughing, like a --
Just insert it --

00:03:30.909 --> 00:03:33.579
-Right, okay.
-You know, make it look like --

00:03:33.579 --> 00:03:35.515
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.515 --> 00:03:38.050
Yes!
That's perfect!

00:03:38.050 --> 00:03:41.254
Perfect, perfect, perfect.
-Now, I would imagine

00:03:41.254 --> 00:03:43.189
you take it seriously --
-I am here,

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:47.426
and I do ride quite a bit.
I haven't had much time

00:03:47.426 --> 00:03:51.297
because, you know --
We'll talk about this project

00:03:51.297 --> 00:03:53.699
I'm involved with, "Worth."

00:03:53.699 --> 00:03:55.735
So I've been, you know,
talking about that a lot.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:04:01.507
But, one of --
speaking of my horses, actually,

00:04:01.507 --> 00:04:04.544
I'm not a --

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:07.947
Not a giant consumer,
you know, of things.

00:04:07.947 --> 00:04:11.784
I don't -- I actually don't
need a lot of stuff,

00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:14.387
which is fortunate,
so then I don't have to go out

00:04:14.387 --> 00:04:16.155
and buy a lot of stuff.

00:04:16.155 --> 00:04:19.292
But one of the things I did
recently, speaking of horses,

00:04:19.292 --> 00:04:22.861
was I kept telling myself,
"Someday, I'm going to

00:04:22.861 --> 00:04:26.699
treat myself --"
which, you know,

00:04:26.699 --> 00:04:27.934
is kind of difficult
for me to do.

00:04:27.934 --> 00:04:29.435
"But I want to treat myself
to something,"

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:31.404
and I thought, "Yeah.
You know what I'm gonna do?

00:04:31.404 --> 00:04:34.874
Instead of all these used,
really nice good saddles I have

00:04:34.874 --> 00:04:37.410
that, you know, I bought
secondhand or a friend of mine

00:04:37.410 --> 00:04:40.212
gave me one as a gift,
I'm gonna treat myself

00:04:40.212 --> 00:04:41.848
to a handmade saddle,"

00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:45.083
which is an art form,
to have a handmade saddle.

00:04:45.083 --> 00:04:51.724
So, I contacted this guy
whose last name is Cooper.

00:04:51.724 --> 00:04:53.292
He's a very good saddle maker.

00:04:53.292 --> 00:04:55.328
Out of Texas,
moved to California.

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:57.497
He started to work on my saddle.

00:04:57.497 --> 00:04:59.198
And that takes --
You know, that's a process.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:01.233
So, we started.
I went up to his shop,

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:02.601
and I said,
"Yeah, let's get started."

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.069
Then we would e-mail.

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:06.805
We would e-mail
one another back and forth.

00:05:06.805 --> 00:05:09.542
And it gets
in the minutia, you know?

00:05:09.542 --> 00:05:11.344
It gets pretty esoteric

00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:12.778
unless you know
what you're talking about.

00:05:12.778 --> 00:05:15.648
So we'd go back and forth.
I'd go, "Hey, man.

00:05:15.648 --> 00:05:18.217
You know,
in terms of the saddle horn,

00:05:18.217 --> 00:05:20.286
you know what I think?
I think we need a 3/4

00:05:20.286 --> 00:05:22.221
instead of a half inch,"
blah, blah, blah.

00:05:22.221 --> 00:05:23.822
"The skirt should be like this.

00:05:23.822 --> 00:05:26.559
I don't want
a full oxbow stirrup.

00:05:26.559 --> 00:05:28.360
Maybe --"
And we're going back and forth.

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:32.265
And he'd respond, and then
there would be these gaps,

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:34.700
and I wouldn't hear from him,
and I'd go,

00:05:34.700 --> 00:05:36.769
"Hey. How you doing?
Just checking in."

00:05:36.769 --> 00:05:38.337
You know, "We were talking about
the engraving

00:05:38.337 --> 00:05:41.607
down on the bottom.
Keep it simple."

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:47.313
"Yeah, okay. Ha, ha!"
Exclamation point, right?

00:05:47.313 --> 00:05:50.883
And I'm going,
"All right, you know.

00:05:50.883 --> 00:05:53.219
You know, he's enjoying this."

00:05:53.219 --> 00:05:54.921
Then I wouldn't
hear from him for a while.

00:05:54.921 --> 00:05:57.757
And I'd go, "Hey,
just checking in," you know?

00:05:57.757 --> 00:06:00.125
"What do you think about
the blah, blah, blah."

00:06:00.125 --> 00:06:03.296
And he'd go "Sure."
So I'm like, "Okay, whatever."

00:06:03.296 --> 00:06:06.699
I'd go, "Whatever?"
[ Laughter ]

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:08.468
Right?
Right?

00:06:08.468 --> 00:06:10.936
That's my boy, Cash Cooper.

00:06:10.936 --> 00:06:13.339
And I'd go, "Hey, I haven't
heard from you in a while."

00:06:13.339 --> 00:06:15.575
"Yeah,"
and then there'd be nothing.

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:20.680
And then, one day,
I e-mailed him something to --

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:23.648
"Let's wrap this up,"
because admittedly,

00:06:23.648 --> 00:06:25.251
I was taking a long time.

00:06:25.251 --> 00:06:30.989
And then, I hear -- he e-mails
me back, "Who is this?"

00:06:30.989 --> 00:06:33.225
I go, "'Who is this?'

00:06:33.225 --> 00:06:36.529
What are you talking about,
'Who is this?'"

00:06:36.529 --> 00:06:39.999
I had been mistakenly e-mailing
Bradley Cooper.

00:06:39.999 --> 00:06:41.501
[ Laughter ]

00:06:41.501 --> 00:06:45.805
Yeah.
The whole time --

00:06:45.805 --> 00:06:47.473
And he's on the other end going,

00:06:47.473 --> 00:06:48.807
"What the [bleep]
is he even talking --

00:06:48.807 --> 00:06:51.344
I don't even know
what this means."

00:06:51.344 --> 00:06:55.781
And then he --
I thought it was hysterical.

00:06:55.781 --> 00:06:58.351
And I e-mailed him back,
and I go, "Oh, man,

00:06:58.351 --> 00:07:00.185
that is so funny,"
blah, blah, blah.

00:07:00.185 --> 00:07:02.889
And he goes, "Yeah, I was kind
of rolling along with it,

00:07:02.889 --> 00:07:07.026
but I didn't know
what I was supposed to say."

00:07:07.026 --> 00:07:10.162
And he said, "I thought it was
really a great gag,

00:07:10.162 --> 00:07:12.831
but I ran out --
I kind of ran out, you know,

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:14.166
because you were getting
really specific..."

00:07:14.166 --> 00:07:17.736
[ Laughter ]
"...about stuff."

00:07:17.736 --> 00:07:20.205
-That's fantastic!
I love that.

00:07:20.205 --> 00:07:22.341
You did put a unfair pressure
on him that he's like,

00:07:22.341 --> 00:07:25.143
"Okay. We're doing a bit.
I don't understand it."

00:07:25.143 --> 00:07:28.314
[ Laughter ]
-Right, exactly.

00:07:28.314 --> 00:07:32.017
Yeah.
It's just like, "You know..."

00:07:32.017 --> 00:07:33.786
-I do want
to talk about the movie.

00:07:33.786 --> 00:07:35.721
We'll be right back with more
from Michael Keaton.

